 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11), overridden by the maintainer.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit status; the success path
+  still ends in `exit 0`.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e` / `set +e`, or swallowing internal failures with `|| true` / a trailing unconditional
+  `exit 0` that masks them.
+- Consequence: an `[ ... ] && cmd` list as the last command of a loop makes the loop (and, via `pipefail`, the
+  enclosing command substitution) return non-zero when the test is false, so such tests are written as `if` blocks.
+- Known risk (accepted): the 2026-09-10 incident — a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and
+  blocked every commit in the repository for a day — can recur, since the check now blocks on its own failure.
+  The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard narrows but does not close that race.
+
+### History
+
+#### §check-fails-open (superseded 2026-10-23)
 - Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
+- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ended in `exit 0`; the script only warned.
 - Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check was advisory by design.
